## 2026-10-17 — F02 real Keycloak token → AIP session attempt (not passed)

Environment: isolated Keycloak 26.2.5 TLS test realm at `https://127.0.0.1:58094`, dedicated AIP PostgreSQL 14.18 on `127.0.0.1:55439` (database `aip_flaskbb`, schema `aip_flaskbb_auth`), actual `target/debug/aip service`, official Python SDK from `product/python`, synthetic test user `auth_new`. No production identity, account, database, mail, or secret was used. The SDK request was from a real Keycloak-signed token obtained from the isolated test user's password grant (grant enabled only in this test client); it was not a browser authorization-code flow.

Commands/actions and observed output:
- `python3 /private/tmp/flaskbb_keycloak_token.py` → `token_typ=at+jwt`; `issuer=https://127.0.0.1:58094/realms/flaskbb-port`; `audience_match=true`; `lifetime_seconds=300`. The token subject was the synthetic test user's Keycloak subject; no token bytes or credentials are recorded here.
- `env SSL_CERT_FILE=... AIP_FLASKBB_DATABASE=... target/debug/aip service serve --config /private/tmp/flaskbb-keycloak-aip-service.json porting/flaskbb/app.aip` → listening on `127.0.0.1:58083`, `authentication=jwt-access-token`, `ok=true`.
- Operator binding of the synthetic subject to seeded fixture actor 6 returned `{"affected":1,"ok":true}`.
- Official SDK `client._describe(token)` → exception `aip_sdk.AipError: WIRE.UNAUTHENTICATED`. AIP server request log: `{"code":"WIRE.UNAUTHENTICATED","event":"request","method":"POST","ms":3,"requestId":"req_0961e251afd8c4114cef7787","route":"session","status":200,...}`. No capability or User read followed because session failed.
- Fresh Keycloak token payload claim inspection (values omitted except contract): header has `alg=RS256`, `typ=at+jwt`, and a key id matching the live JWKS; payload has HTTPS `iss`, `aud` including `aip-flaskbb`, `sub`, `iat`, `exp` (300 seconds), but no `nbf`. Downloaded public JWKS equals the HTTPS Keycloak JWKS response. AIP verifier source `crates/aip-auth/src/lib.rs` requires `iss`, `aud`, `sub`, `exp`, and `nbf` (`set_required_spec_claims`); session lookup then requires the exact issuer/subject principal mapping.

Assessment: this is an actual IdP-issued signed token, but not a successful AIP login. The missing required `nbf` is the leading source-supported explanation for `WIRE.UNAUTHENTICATED` (the external response deliberately does not distinguish JWT validation causes). This remains an inferred cause, not an instrumented verifier trace. One initial service start used an incorrect local database role and returned `DB_CONNECT`; it was corrected to the test role and does not describe product or production availability. A prior remote JWKS HTTPS source attempt returned generic `AUTH_CONFIG`; current service used the JWKS fetched over HTTPS from the same test IdP and read from a local file. Remote JWKS TLS loading therefore remains unverified. `SSL_CERT_FILE` was present in the AIP service environment.

Next gates: resolve supported IdP/AIP JWT claim compatibility without weakening signature/issuer/audience checks; repeat real Keycloak token → SDK `/session` → authorized AIP read; provide a safe product path for self-registered IdP subject → AIP actor provisioning (operator CLI bind alone is not that path); test authorization-code/PKCE browser flow and logout. F01/F05 source UI → AIP SDK and F03–F17 remain open.
